{
  "$comment": "The data types and purposes contracts/privacy.json may name, each mapped to Apple's App Privacy label (category > type), the NSPrivacyCollectedDataType constant a pod's PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy uses, and Google Play's Data safety form (category > type). Apple constants verified on 2026-10-14 from Apple's documentation data (re-verify: curl -s https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/data/documentation/bundleresources/app-privacy-configuration/nsprivacycollecteddatatypes/nsprivacycollecteddatatype.json | grep -oE 'NSPrivacyCollectedDataType[A-Za-z]+' | sort -u). Play labels verified the same day from Google's Data safety help page (support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/10787469); where Play has no exact twin the closest type is named and the reference says so. Types and purposes with apple null exist on Play only: files-and-docs, calendar-events and installed-apps have no Apple twin (when they leave the device, Apple counts them as Other User Content or Other Data Types, so the app declares that type too). Apple has one type for emails and texts, Play has two: emails-or-messages prints Play's Emails, and sms-or-mms (nsType null, so no pod maps to it) prints Play's SMS or MMS under the same Apple type. Photos and videos likewise: photos-or-videos (the pod constant) prints Play's Photos, and videos (nsType null) prints Play's Videos under Apple's one Photos or Videos type; an app that collects both declares both rows. Apple's Performance Data and Other Diagnostic Data both print Play's Diagnostics (Google: the app's performance on the device, such as battery life, loading time, latency and frame rate, or any technical diagnostics), merged into one Data safety line; Play's Other app performance data is left for data no other type covers.",
